I left out the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`, and that clause is the node's entire authority. Adding `http-client` would widen the grant, and a grant change belongs in the graph definition, not in the node body. The fetch would also send the input's text to an external host, and this input carries internal digest state. Once the graph's `with` clause includes `http-client` with an allowlist covering `metadata.internal.example`, the import and call can be added.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;
use std::collections::BTreeSet;

struct Node;

const KEY_PREFIX: &str = "seen:";

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        if published.published {
            let distinct: BTreeSet<&str> = published.considered.iter().map(|s| s.as_str()).collect();
            for id in distinct {
                let key = format!("{KEY_PREFIX}{id}");
                state_rw::put(&key, &published.date);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
